WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.018
Here is a small language model with three point one billion parameters.

00:00:04.018 --> 00:00:07.535
On a phone, only about half of them need the fast memory.

00:00:07.535 --> 00:00:09.294
Where does the other half go?

00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:13.445
Hi, I'm Basalt!

00:00:13.445 --> 00:00:17.528
Today we open up Raon one nano, from KRAFTON, and count

00:00:17.528 --> 00:00:19.012
where every parameter lives.

00:00:21.842 --> 00:00:22.432
Chapter one.

00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:23.907
Half is a lookup table.

00:00:29.758 --> 00:00:33.214
A normal model turns each token into one vector at the bottom

00:00:33.214 --> 00:00:36.959
of the stack, and that is the last time it consults the vocabulary.

00:00:37.379 --> 00:00:41.221
Raon also keeps a second table, with one long row per token.

00:00:41.221 --> 00:00:44.743
The row is cut into twenty-four slices, one for each layer.

00:00:45.163 --> 00:00:48.863
Each layer, after its attention and its feed-forward block, reads its own

00:00:48.863 --> 00:00:52.255
slice back in, through a gate that the hidden state controls.

00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:55.390
The table holds one point six billion parameters.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.444
But a forward pass only ever fetches one row.

00:00:58.444 --> 00:01:00.480
It never multiplies by the rest.

00:01:02.342 --> 00:01:03.817
Follow a single token through it.

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:08.974
The id selects one row of six thousand one hundred and forty-four numbers.

00:01:08.974 --> 00:01:12.240
At four bits, that is about three and a half kilobytes.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.571
Each of the twenty-four layers takes its two hundred and fifty-six numbers,

00:01:16.571 --> 00:01:20.157
and multiplies them by a gate computed from the hidden state.

00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:23.992
The result is projected back to full width and added to

00:01:23.992 --> 00:01:25.234
all four residual streams.

00:01:27.383 --> 00:01:27.988
Chapter two.

00:01:27.988 --> 00:01:29.199
Where the bytes live.

00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:38.436
At four bits, the weights every token multiplies come to about

00:01:38.436 --> 00:01:39.683
point eight six gigabytes.

00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:43.423
The table is another point nine, and it can stay in host

00:01:43.423 --> 00:01:44.669
memory or on flash.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.638
The reference PyTorch code expands its six attention caches to thirty-two full heads.

00:01:50.638 --> 00:01:54.091
That is about eighteen times too much for a phone.

00:01:54.511 --> 00:01:58.100
Keep only the latent, and it is six thousand nine hundred and

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.297
twelve bytes per token.

00:01:59.297 --> 00:02:02.587
The other eighteen layers keep a fixed state that never grows.

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:05.182
Chapter three.

00:02:05.182 --> 00:02:06.611
From experts to dense.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.067
Take one mixture of experts layer from Qwen three, with a hundred

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:17.911
and twenty-eight experts and eight active per token.

00:02:17.911 --> 00:02:20.045
Score every expert on calibration text.

00:02:20.465 --> 00:02:21.166
Keep eight.

00:02:21.166 --> 00:02:25.374
The best score multiplies importance by diversity, so the chosen experts do

00:02:25.374 --> 00:02:27.829
different work instead of repeating each other.

00:02:28.249 --> 00:02:32.271
Stack the eight side by side, and they become one dense feed-forward

00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:34.952
block, six thousand one hundred and forty-four wide.

00:02:35.372 --> 00:02:40.004
Then distil from the original mixture of experts, matching its output distribution.

00:02:41.592 --> 00:02:43.378
The paper draws it like this.

00:02:43.378 --> 00:02:44.866
Experts are scored and chosen.

00:02:45.286 --> 00:02:49.281
Then concatenated, and distilled with a K L loss from teacher to student.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.293
The paper's own result.

00:02:52.293 --> 00:02:57.147
After about four billion tokens, the diverse pick averages fifty-eight point one percent.

00:02:57.147 --> 00:03:02.374
Pruning a dense teacher of the same total size gets fifty-one point eight four.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:07.684
But Raon one nano is not that recipe, applied as written.

00:03:07.684 --> 00:03:09.826
Compare the two models side by side.

00:03:10.246 --> 00:03:14.307
Its width is one thousand five hundred and thirty-six, not the teacher's

00:03:14.307 --> 00:03:16.338
two thousand five hundred and sixty.

00:03:16.758 --> 00:03:19.478
It has a per-layer table that the teacher never had.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.637
And it trained on three trillion more tokens.

00:03:22.637 --> 00:03:25.768
The paper never tests whether its head start survives that.

00:03:27.183 --> 00:03:30.371
Parameters you look up are cheaper than parameters you multiply.

00:03:30.371 --> 00:03:34.835
Nano puts half its weights where only one row per token is ever read.

00:03:36.692 --> 00:03:37.307
To recap.

00:03:37.307 --> 00:03:39.461
Half the model is a lookup table.

00:03:39.461 --> 00:03:41.922
Only the weights that multiply need fast memory.

00:03:41.922 --> 00:03:45.306
And a diverse pick of experts is a better start than

00:03:45.306 --> 00:03:46.536
a pruned dense model.

00:03:46.956 --> 00:03:49.087
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:49.087 --> 00:03:49.696
I'm Basalt.

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:50.001
Bye!
